.TH LOGIN 1 2026-10-18 "Peios authd 0.0.24"
.SH NAME
login \- start a Peios terminal logon session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B login
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-H ]
.RB [ \-h
.IR host ]
.RI [ username ]
.br
.B login
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-H ]
.RB [ \-h
.IR host ]
.BR \-\-try " " username
.br
.B login
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-H ]
.RB [ \-h
.IR host ]
.BR \-\-try-no-password " " username
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B login
collects credentials through the terminal, asks the Peios authentication
authority for a primary token, installs the granted token on itself, and
replaces itself with the principal's login shell.
.PP
With no
.I username
it prompts for one.  Supplying a name selects that principal directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-console
Prepare a managed console: restore canonical input, echo and standard control
characters while preserving serial speed, and wait until first-boot setup has
retired both of its service definitions before accepting credentials. Registry
errors fail closed. This option grants no authentication authority.
When GXWI is installed, show its configured browser address and the public
certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint before authentication. Unavailable browser
information does not prevent login. The information is read once per invocation.
.TP
.B \-p
Preserve the inherited environment instead of constructing a fresh login
environment. Locale preferences are still refreshed from the registry;
inherited LC_ALL and LANGUAGE are removed.
.TP
.B \-H
Accept the conventional request to suppress a hostname banner.  The current
client emits no such banner, so this option has no further effect.
.TP
.BI \-h " host"
Record the remote peer on whose behalf this interactive logon is originated.
.TP
.BI \-\-try " username"
Attempt the named principal first and fall back to the normal prompt after a
non-fatal denial.
.TP
.BI \-\-try-no-password " username"
Attempt the named principal while advertising that the client can collect no
credentials.  If authentication requires interaction, fall back silently to
the normal prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing.
.SH LOCALE
New sessions read LANG and individual LC_* preferences from
Machine\eSystem\eLocale, then Users\e<SID>\eLocale for the granted principal.
The fallback is C.UTF-8. Invalid or uninstalled locales retain the previous
default. LC_ALL is not a stored preference. Changes apply at the next login;
applications inherit the session environment.
.SH SECURITY
The traditional
.B \-f
authentication bypass is deliberately unsupported.  Authentication decisions
are made by the authority from the verified peer and cannot be asserted by a
command-line flag.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Without
.BR \-p ,
the client clears the inherited environment and sets HOME, SHELL, USER,
LOGNAME, and PATH from the selected profile and account. PATH is /bin.
TERM is preserved when set; otherwise it is chosen from the terminal device,
using linux for a virtual console and vt220 for other terminals.
Locale preferences are refreshed from session policy even with
.BR \-p ;
inherited LC_ALL, LANGUAGE, and locale-category overrides are removed first.
.PP
The shell is executed with a leading dash on its basename in argv[0].
A missing or relative home falls back to /; a missing or relative shell
falls back to /bin/sh. A home that cannot be entered is reported and the
session starts in /.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The client exits with status 1 for an argument error, denied or failed logon,
or failure to execute the shell. A successful execution replaces the client,
so its eventual exit status belongs to the shell.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR authd (8),
.BR lps (1),
.BR lpsd (8)
